# Normalise line endings on checkout.
#
# Without this file, `core.autocrlf=true` — the Git for Windows default, and what
# the windows-latest runner uses — rewrites every text file to CRLF in the working
# tree. That is invisible in a diff, because git normalises on the way back in, but
# it is not invisible to tooling: `npm run version:check` failed in the installer
# workflow with
#
#   src-tauri/Cargo.lock: inventory-gear version "undefined" != package.json "1.0.0-alpha.3"
#
# because the version script matched `name = "inventory-gear"\nversion = "..."`, a
# pattern that cannot match a CRLF file. The same commit checked clean on Linux and
# macOS, so the drift existed only on the machine that gates releases.
#
# `text=auto` lets git work out what is binary, so the screenshots and other assets
# are left alone; `eol=lf` then pins the endings for everything it calls text. This
# also matters for the 8 shell scripts in the repo: a CRLF shebang makes them fail
# outright on Linux with "bad interpreter", and those are run from CI.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The version files are compared and rewritten byte-wise, so they must never pick
# up the platform's endings. Redundant with the rule above in practice, but stated
# because breaking them breaks the release gate rather than a single build.
package.json            text eol=lf
package-lock.json       text eol=lf
src-tauri/Cargo.toml    text eol=lf
src-tauri/Cargo.lock    text eol=lf
src-tauri/tauri.conf.json text eol=lf

# Spreadsheets are zip archives. `text=auto` already detects them as binary, but
# there is no reason to leave that to a heuristic on a file we commit.
*.xlsx binary
